Author: Qiyuan Xu <xqyww123@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Sep 22 14:19:52 2026 +0800

    One renderer: FileIndex takes its unicode offsets from pretty_unicode's own substitution
    
    "What does this symbol render as" was implemented twice: pretty_unicode's two
    regex passes and a symbol-by-symbol loop in FileIndex that redid the table
    lookup and the fold. Rule D44 (a private-use symbol keeps its escape) reached
    only the first, so every hover/definition column after a phi-system keyword
    symbol was off by the escape's length minus one (94, today 107, phi-system
    theories).
    
    Now one private core, _render, runs the two passes exactly as before and
    records (start, end, output length) per match; pretty_unicode is its first
    component, byte-identical on the 4,506-file corpus; pretty_unicode_indexed
    takes symbol_explode's output and maps each symbol's start through the two
    passes' records, so the CR-folded text an index holds is the only text it can
    render. FileIndex hands over the symbols it already exploded and decides
    nothing about rendering.
    
    Also on these lines: the table is cached as one record that
    get_SYMBOLS_AND_REVERSED() projects its unchanged 4-tuple out of (the parallel
    SYMBOL_FILES_CACHE global goes); the len(char) == 1 guard that could not fail
    goes; _load_symbols no longer accumulates across calls through mutable
    defaults.
    
    Tests: twelve seeded rendering classes with expected offsets, a per-line sweep
    of phi-system that must see every class (68,148 lines), and per-file mutants
    for both modules (nine, all killed) including FileIndex restored to its own
    computation; the suite fails against the parent commit's FileIndex (7,571 of
    those lines). Plan UNICODE_CONSISTENCY_FIX_PLAN.md, section 8, steps 1-7.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

